\title{Beyond Shift Magnitude: What Makes a Fine-Tuning Shift Useful for ASR?}
\author{Anonymous Authors}

\newcommand{\blank}{\mathtt{blank}}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Fine-tuning a self-supervised speech model changes its representations, but a large change need not be a useful one for downstream recognition. We investigate this distinction in fine-tuning-induced SSL representation shifts and propose Decision-Aligned Delta Selection (DADS). DADS ranks Delta coordinates by their signed contribution to the margin between a CTC-aligned target token and its strongest competing token, then trains a fresh CTC fusion model using the selected coordinates. Layer scanning supplies a complementary diagnostic: shift magnitude need not predict fusion utility. This formulation tests whether decision utility, rather than magnitude or indiscriminate use of all shifts, identifies the adaptation directions that matter for ASR.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

Fine-tuning adapts a pretrained self-supervised learning (SSL) encoder to a target ASR domain. The resulting change in representation space can provide information complementary to the fine-tuned representation of a second model. Mind the Shift~\citep{wang2026mind} defines a \emph{delta embedding} as the difference between fine-tuned and pretrained representations and shows that such shifts can improve SSL feature fusion. However, treating all shifts as equally useful leaves two questions unanswered: which layer's shift should be fused, and which dimensions within that shift support recognition decisions?

Our central scientific question is: \emph{when fine-tuning changes an SSL representation, which changes actually matter for ASR?} We study this question through the following hypothesis:
\begin{equation}
\text{fine-tuning shift magnitude} \neq \text{ASR fusion utility}.
\end{equation}
At the layer level, we test whether normalized shift magnitude predicts held-out fusion WER. Within a fixed Delta layer, we test whether coordinate magnitude predicts decision utility. DADS uses a full-Delta CTC model to identify coordinates that consistently enlarge the aligned correct token's margin over its strongest actual competitor; a newly initialized CTC head is then trained on only the selected coordinates.

\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

\subsection{Problem setup and delta embeddings}

We use WavLM-Large as the reference SSL encoder and Wav2Vec2-Large-lv60 (W2V2) as the delta encoder. Both encoders have $L=24$ transformer layers and hidden dimension $d=1024$. They are independently fine-tuned for character-level CTC ASR on the same target-domain training set. For an input waveform $x$, let $E^{\mathrm{WavLM}}_{\mathrm{ft}}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}$ denote the final-layer representation of fine-tuned WavLM. Let $E^{\mathrm{W2V2},(l)}_{\mathrm{pt}}$ and $E^{\mathrm{W2V2},(l)}_{\mathrm{ft}}\in\mathbb{R}^{T\times d}$ denote the pretrained and fine-tuned W2V2 representations at layer $l$, respectively. We define the layer-wise delta embedding as
\begin{equation}
\Delta^{(l)}=E^{\mathrm{W2V2},(l)}_{\mathrm{ft}}-E^{\mathrm{W2V2},(l)}_{\mathrm{pt}},
\qquad l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}.
\label{eq:delta}
\end{equation}

The models use convolutional feature encoders with the same effective 20-ms stride. Thus, for the same 16-kHz waveform, their representations share frame index $t$ and can be concatenated directly; no additional temporal alignment module is introduced.

\subsection{Stage I: layer-level fusion utility}
\label{sec:layer-utility}

For every W2V2 layer $l$, we form
\begin{equation}
Z_t^{(l)}=\left[E^{\mathrm{WavLM}}_{\mathrm{ft},t};\Delta_t^{(l)}\right],
\label{eq:layer-fusion}
\end{equation}
where $[\cdot;\cdot]$ denotes feature concatenation. All SSL encoders are frozen during fusion training. For source-compatible baseline reproduction, we independently initialize and train one $\operatorname{LayerNorm}\rightarrow\operatorname{Dropout}\rightarrow\operatorname{Linear}$ CTC readout $\mathrm{CTC}^{(l)}$ for each layer using $D_{\mathrm{train}}$. Its development-set WER fixes $l^*$:
\begin{equation}
l^*=\arg\min_{l\in\{1,\ldots,L\}}
\operatorname{WER}_{D_{\mathrm{dev}}}
\left(\mathrm{CTC}^{(l)}(Z^{(l)})\right).
\label{eq:best-layer}
\end{equation}
This selection is intentionally based on recognition performance, not on the size of the representation shift.

For analysis, we additionally measure the normalized shift magnitude at each layer:
\begin{equation}
S_l=\mathbb{E}_{x,t}\left[
\frac{\lVert\Delta_t^{(l)}\rVert_2}
{\lVert E^{\mathrm{W2V2},(l)}_{\mathrm{pt},t}\rVert_2+\epsilon}
\right].
\label{eq:shift-magnitude}
\end{equation}
Comparing $\arg\max_l S_l$ with $l^*$ is a diagnostic analysis rather than a claimed contribution: it tests whether the layer that changes most is also the layer that is most useful for fusion.

\subsection{Stage II: decision-level feature utility}
\label{sec:feature-utility}

Having selected $l^*$, we write $\Delta=\Delta^{(l^*)}$ and train a new, frozen-encoder, pure-linear full-Delta attribution teacher $\mathrm{CTC}_{\mathrm{full}}$ on
\begin{equation}
h_t=\left[E^{\mathrm{WavLM}}_{\mathrm{ft},t};\Delta_t\right],
\qquad z_t=Wh_t+b.
\label{eq:full-ctc}
\end{equation}
Here $z_t$ contains logits for the complete CTC vocabulary. Partitioning the linear head by its input gives $W=[W^{\mathrm{ref}};W^\Delta]$, where $W^\Delta$ reads the $d$ delta dimensions.
The pure-linear attribution teacher is intentionally distinct from the source-compatible baseline readout. A LayerNorm readout couples input coordinates, whereas the linear teacher makes the coordinate decomposition below exact.


For transcript $y$, we obtain a Viterbi forced CTC alignment under $\mathrm{CTC}_{\mathrm{full}}$:
\begin{equation}
\pi^*=\arg\max_{\pi:\,B(\pi)=y}P(\pi\mid x),
\qquad a_t=\pi_t^*,
\label{eq:viterbi}
\end{equation}
where $B(\cdot)$ is the standard CTC collapse operator. We evaluate utility only on character-aligned frames,
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{A}=\{t:a_t\neq\blank\},\qquad N=|\mathcal{A}|.
\label{eq:active-frames}
\end{equation}
Repeated aligned frames are retained because each supplies acoustic support for its target character. At each such frame, the strongest incorrect competitor is
\begin{equation}
c_t=\arg\max_{k\neq a_t}z_{t,k}.
\label{eq:competitor}
\end{equation}
The maximization ranges over the full vocabulary, including $\blank$; blank is often the dominant CTC alternative to a target character.

Let $\delta_{t,i}$ be the $i$th component of $\Delta_t$. Its signed contribution to the target-versus-competitor logit margin is
\begin{equation}
q_{t,i}=\delta_{t,i}\left(W^\Delta_{a_t,i}-W^\Delta_{c_t,i}\right).
The competitor is selected from the full teacher logits and held fixed for this decomposition. Thus $q_{t,i}$ is an exact local attribution to the observed target-versus-competitor margin, not a claim that the competitor would remain unchanged after masking a coordinate.

\label{eq:decision-contribution}
\end{equation}
When $q_{t,i}>0$, dimension $i$ increases the correct-token margin; when $q_{t,i}<0$, it increases the competing token's relative margin. We aggregate this directional evidence across all aligned decisions:
\begin{equation}
U_i=\frac{1}{N}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{A}}\operatorname{sign}(q_{t,i})
=r_i^{\mathrm{help}}-r_i^{\mathrm{harm}},
\label{eq:feature-utility}
\end{equation}
where $r_i^{\mathrm{help}}$ and $r_i^{\mathrm{harm}}$ are the fractions of positive and negative contributions, respectively. Hence, $U_i\in[-1,1]$ measures whether a delta dimension consistently helps, harms, or has no stable directional effect on CTC decisions.

\subsection{Utility-selected delta fusion}
\label{sec:selection}

For a feature budget $K$, let $\mathcal{I}_K$ be the indices of the $K$ largest utilities. We define
\begin{equation}
M_i^{\mathrm{utility}}=
\begin{cases}
1, & i\in\mathcal{I}_K,\\
0, & \text{otherwise},
\end{cases}
\qquad
\mathcal{I}_K=\operatorname{TopK}(\{U_i\}_{i=1}^{d}).
\label{eq:mask}
\end{equation}
The final representation is
\begin{equation}
Z_t^{\mathrm{sel}}=
\left[E^{\mathrm{WavLM}}_{\mathrm{ft},t};
M^{\mathrm{utility}}\odot\Delta_t\right].
\label{eq:selected-fusion}
\end{equation}
Crucially, we discard $\mathrm{CTC}_{\mathrm{full}}$ after ranking: a newly initialized pure-linear head $\mathrm{CTC}_{\mathrm{sel}}$ is trained on $Z^{\mathrm{sel}}$. The final result therefore tests the utility-selected representation rather than the effect of pruning an already trained classifier.

\section{Experimental Protocol}
\label{sec:protocol}

All model-selection decisions use only the training and development splits. We train the $24$ layer-specific heads on $D_{\mathrm{train}}$ and choose $l^*$ with development WER. We reserve a fixed utility-estimation subset from training data, train $\mathrm{CTC}_{\mathrm{full}}$ on the remaining training examples, and compute the utility ranking only on the reserved subset. The test set is used only for the final frozen evaluation.

We evaluate $K\in\{32,64,128,256,512,1024\}$, corresponding to $3.125\%$, $6.25\%$, $12.5\%$, $25\%$, $50\%$, and $100\%$ of the 1024-dimensional delta. In addition to utility selection, we compare:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Final-layer full delta}: the original final-layer fusion $[E^{\mathrm{WavLM}}_{\mathrm{ft},L};\Delta^{(L)}]$;
\item \textbf{Best-layer full delta}: the full delta at $l^*$;
\item \textbf{Random-$K$}: $K$ randomly selected delta dimensions; and
\item \textbf{Magnitude-$K$}: the $K$ dimensions with largest $m_i=\mathbb{E}_{x,t}|\delta_{t,i}|$.
\end{enumerate}
These comparisons test the intended evidence chain: the largest layer shift need not be the best fusion layer; the largest per-feature shift need not be the most useful feature; and a compact set of decision-aligned dimensions may outperform both random or magnitude-based selections and the full delta.

\section{Conclusion}

DADS reframes Delta embedding fusion as a decision-utility problem. After a diagnostic layer scan fixes the Delta layer, it selects coordinates through their stable contribution to CTC target margins. The resulting decision-aligned Delta representation isolates the shifts that are most useful for SSL-based ASR feature fusion.

